sec.p:
If the environment is Markovian, V (st) can be expressed recursively as:
(2)
V(st)=E[rt+1+γV(st+1)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V\left(s_{t}\right)=\mathbb{E}\left[r_{t+1}+\gamma V\left(s_{t+1}\right)\right] \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The TD algorithm exploits this recursion using an online learning rule where value estimates are updated in light of immediate rewards, and differences in value between successive states. This is formalised in a teaching signal called the reward prediction error δt, defined as:
(3)
δt=rt+1+γV(st+1)−V(st) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \delta_{t}=r_{t+1}+\gamma V\left(s_{t+1}\right)-V\left(s_{t}\right) \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
In this equation, the estimate of the old state’s value V (st) is a prediction – i.e., it is the current ‘best guess’ about expected future reward – while the immediate reward rt+1 plus the discounted value estimate for the new state V (st+1) is an update target – i.e. the value toward which the prediction is adjusted. Value estimates are updated using RPEs as:
(4)
V(st)←V(st)+αδt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V\left(s_{t}\right) \leftarrow V\left(s_{t}\right)+\alpha \delta_{t} \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Where α ∈ [0, 1] is a learning rate controlling how value estimates change in response to RPEs. The TD algorithm is the basis of an influential account of value-learning in cortico-striatal circuits (Montague et al., 1996; Schultz et al., 1997). This account has three main components fig. 1A-i: (i) The cortex constructs a state representation from sensory experience and communicates it to the striatum (Chang and Tsao, 2017; Liu et al., 2016; Yamins and DiCarlo, 2016); (ii) the striatum estimates value using cortico-striatal synaptic weights, which reflect the relationship between state-features and value (Samejima et al., 2005; Van Der Meer, 2009), and; (iii) VTA dopaminergic neurons compute reward prediction errors that induce plasticity at cortico-striatal synapses (Pawlak and Kerr, 2008), enabling stored values to be updated.

If the model-based system can predict future rewards that are not yet reflected in cached values, it can be used to train cached values via the RPE. We argue that to accomplish this, inferred values should contribute to the RPE as:
(5)
VNET(st+1)=kVMB(st+1)+(1−k)VTD(st+1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V_{N E T}\left(s_{t+1}\right)=k V_{M B}\left(s_{t+1}\right)+(1-k) V_{T D}\left(s_{t+1}\right) \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(6)
δt=rt+1+γVNET(st+1)−VTD(st) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \delta_{t}=r_{t+1}+\gamma V_{N E T}\left(s_{t+1}\right)-V_{T D}\left(s_{t}\right) \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Here, the estimated value of the new state VNET (St+1) used in the update target combines an inferred value estimate VMB and a cached value estimate VTD using a mixing parameter k. Although our model is agnostic as to how k is determined, it should in principal ensure that VNET reflects the agent’s best estimate of the true value given the reliability of VTD and VMB e.g., using uncertainty- or confidence-based arbitration (Daw et al., 2005; Lee et al., 2014). We do not provide a substantive account of arbitration in this paper and our simulations use a fixed value of k = 0.5 for simplicity (see Methods). Cached values are then updated using the RPE as:
(7)
VTD(s)←VTD(s)+αTDδt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V_{T D}(s) \leftarrow V_{T D}(s)+\alpha_{T D} \delta_{t} \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Where αTD is a learning-rate for cached values.

Following Mikhael et al. (2022), we assume the subject does not know the true current state st of the environment, but instead maintains a probability distribution p(s| xt) over possible states s given sensory input x. This probability distribution is assumed to be a Gaussian centred on st with standard deviation σt:
(10)
p(s∣xt)∼N(st,σt) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(s \mid x_{t}\right) \sim \mathcal{N}\left(s_{t}, \sigma_{t}\right) \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Value estimates with respect to x are computed as a probability weighted sum over state value estimates:
(11)
VTDx∼∑sp(s∣x)VTDs \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V_{T D}^{x} \sim \sum_{s} p(s \mid x) V_{T D}^{s} \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(12)
VMBx∼∑sp(s∣x)VMBs \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V_{M B}^{x} \sim \sum_{s} p(s \mid x) V_{M B}^{s} \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Simulating this version of the model on standard trials with constant state uncertainty produced ramping RPEs consistent with those observed without state uncertainty (fig. 5C). Following Mikhael et al. (2022), darkening trials were modelled by gradually increasing the width of the state uncertainty kernel across the trial. This generated RPE bumps similar to the dopamine bumps seen in the experimental data (fig. 5C).

To characterise whether the dual-process model accelerated value-learning (fig. 1B), we implemented (i) a dual-process agent, (ii) a standard TD agent, and (iii) an alternative dual-process agent on a linear track environment. The standard TD agents was implemented according to eqs. 3—4. The alternative dual process agent was implemented according to:
(13)
VNET(s)=kVTD(s)+(1−k)VMB(s) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V_{N E T}(s)=k V_{T D}(s)+(1-k) V_{M B}(s) \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(14)
δt=rt+1+γVNET(st+1)−VNET(st) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \delta_{t}=r_{t+1}+\gamma V_{N E T}\left(s_{t+1}\right)-V_{N E T}\left(s_{t}\right) \tag{14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(15)
VTD(s)←VTD(s)+αTDδt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V_{T D}(s) \leftarrow V_{T D}(s)+\alpha_{T D} \delta_{t} \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The key difference compared to the dual process agent defined in eqs. 6—7, therefore, is that inferred values appear in both the RPE update target and the prediction, instead of the update target alone. This alternative dual-process agent learned slower than standard TD learning (fig. 1B) and did not generate ramping RPEs. Parameters for the agents were: αTD = 0.01, αMB = 0.50, γ = 0.93, k = 0.50,
